# Teaches: the arrow REFUSES.
# The tee at r2c1 is LOCKED, so its east mouth permanently stares into o3 - a
# one-way pointing back toward the source. That mouth gets wet and nothing flows
# through it; the arrow SITS SHUT - a sluice bar closed behind the chevron - and says so.
# It is a state, not an event: on 16 and 17 an arrow can stay shut for most of the level
# while the player works out the long way round, so it has to survive being stared at.
# (Not greyed: a refused tile is dry, and the dry channel floor is the same colour a
# greyed arrow would be, so it would vanish exactly when it needs to be read.) The lesson is forced, not optional,
# and it costs the player no moves to see. The only real route is north and around.
#
# Authoring note: my first cut of this level declared par 9 and the validator
# hard-failed it - a 7-move solution existed because the tee was rotatable and
# could reach the goal without ever offering its east mouth. Locking the tee both
# fixes the par and is the better level, because it guarantees the lesson is seen.
id: 14
name: Wrong Way
size: 6x5
par: 7
tide: 13
source: r2c0
grid:
  .. L0 i1 i1 L0 ..
  .. I1 .. .. I1 ..
  e1 t3 o3 i1 L2 e3
  .. .. .. .. .. ..
  .. .. .. .. .. ..
critters:
  r2c5 starfish
solution:
  r1c1 cw1
  r0c1 cw1
  r0c4 cw2
  r1c4 cw1
  r2c4 cw2
